      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Since angiopoietin-2 (Ang-2) levels strongly correlate with cardiovascular mortality and subclinical cardiovascular disease, it was hypothesized that levels of Ang-2 and its soluble receptor (sTie-2) were associated with the metabolic syndrome (MetS) and individual MetS components."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Within the population-based Study of Health in Pomerania, two sets of analyses were performed. First, Ang-2 and sTie-2 were related to the prevalence of MetS and its components cross-sectionally (n\u2009=\u20093,205). Second, the association between baseline Ang-2 and sTie-2 and incident MetS or longitudinal changes in its components in 1,295 individuals was investigated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "High Ang-2 levels (90th percentile), compared with low Ang-2 levels (10th percentile), were positively associated with MetS (OR: 1.78) and with the following MetS criteria: increased triglycerides, lower HDL cholesterol, and higher non-fasting glucose. Furthermore, high sTie-2 levels (90th percentile), compared with low levels (10th percentile), were positively related to MetS (OR: 1.58) and most of its components. However, Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were not associated with incident MetS or longitudinal change in components of MetS."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were cross-sectionally associated with MetS and several of its components. However, Ang-2 and sTie-2 levels were not associated with incident MetS or changes in individual MetS components during follow-up."
          }
        ]
      },
